from ../meaning/Facts import Facts
from ../meaning/Conditions import Conditions
from ../meaning/Resolution import Resolution
from ../model/OpenItems import Contradiction

export anchor StructuralContext:
    description:
        Before analysis reads a single sentence, it already knows a great deal
        about a specification, because Piton structure says it outright. It
        knows which anchors exist, what properties they have, what they
        extend, what they refer to, and what values some properties hold. The
        compiler has already checked all of it.

        So analysis starts with structure, and structure shapes everything it
        does with prose. A sentence is never read on its own. It is read as a
        sentence inside a particular property of a particular anchor, which
        extends particular bases, in a file that imports particular names.

    whatStructureGives:
        ```text
        entities          every top-level declaration, with its keyword, and
                          whether it is abstract or exported
        parts             every property, known by its path, such as Button.disabled
        inheritance       the chain of bases each anchor extends, directly or
                          through its keyword, and which properties it overrides
        references        every @, dollar and brace reference, already resolved
        names in scope    what each file declares, and what it imports, under
                          which alias
        types             every type constraint on a property
        values            every property value that is structure rather than
                          prose, and every value an expression evaluates to
        examples          every code block
        ```

    structuralFacts:
        Much of this becomes facts straight away, with the state structural.

        ```piton
        anchor Control:
            description: Anything a person can operate.

        export anchor Button extends Control:
            label:: string: Save
            color: blue
        ```

        ```text
        Button inherits Control
        Button defines Button.label, Button.color
        Button.label = Save            constrained to string
        Button.color = blue
        ```

        A property whose value is prose does not become a fact this way; its
        prose is read by the steps that follow. What a fact is, and how its
        parts are written, is described in @{Facts}.

    theScopeOfAStatement:
        Every statement is given its scope before its words are looked at.

        ```text
        entity         the anchor it is written in                  Button
        property path  the property it is written in                Button.disabled
        owner          what its pronouns can stand for              Button
        names          every name the statement can refer to without
                       qualification: the entity's parts, including
                       inherited ones, aspects that facts give it, and
                       everything the file declares or imports
        condition      the condition its property stands for, if any
        ```

        How the scope is used to find what words refer to is described in
        @{Resolution}, and how a property can stand for a condition in
        @{Conditions}.

    structureComesFirst:
        When structure and prose could both answer a question, structure
        answers it. An explicit reference beats a name that merely matches. A
        property named disabled tells analysis more about the sentences in it
        than any of those sentences can.

        This is about interpretation, not truth. If structure says the color
        is blue and prose says it is red, structure does not quietly win;
        the two facts disagree, and that is reported as a @{Contradiction}.

    whenTheCompilerFails:
        Structure comes only from files the compiler accepts. A file that does
        not compile contributes its compiler errors, and nothing else, because
        its structure cannot be trusted.
